    <DataNode elementId="AT2G39030-MONOMER" textLabel="L-ornithine N5-acetyltransferase NATA1" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q9ZV05" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="150" centerY="355" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">NATA1 is an ornithine &lt;i&gt;N&lt;/i&gt;&lt;sup&gt;&amp;delta;&lt;/sup&gt;-acetyltransferase based on the phenotype of &lt;i&gt;nata1-1&lt;/i&gt; mutants. They are unable to produce |FRAME: CPD-13644 L-&lt;i&gt;N&lt;sup&gt;&amp;delta;&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;/i&gt;-acetylornithine|, however, this activity is returned if NATA1 is over-expressed in the mutant background. Furthermore, expression of NATA1 also leads to the production of |FRAME: CPD-13644 L-&lt;i&gt;N&lt;sup&gt;&amp;delta;&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;/i&gt;-acetylornithine| in &lt;i&gt;Nicotiana benthamiana&lt;/i&gt; |CITS: [21917546]|. NATA1 transcripts levels are elevated more than 20-fold 1.5 hours after treatment with the volatile phytohormone |FRAME: Methyl-Jasmonates methyl jasmonate| (MeJA). Within several days after the treatment, NATA1 levels fell to their initial levels. Accumulation of |FRAME: CPD-13644 L-&lt;i&gt;N&lt;sup&gt;&amp;delta;&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;/i&gt;-acetylornithine| appears to follow these changes in transcript level |CITS: [21917546]|. NATA1 transcript levels also increase in response to the phloem-feeding green peach aphid &lt;i&gt;Myzus persicae&lt;/i&gt;. |FRAME: CPD-13644 L-&lt;i&gt;N&lt;sup&gt;&amp;delta;&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;/i&gt;-acetylornithine|, which can be detected in Arabidopsis phloem exudates, decreases the reproduction of this pest, suggesting that it may serve as a defense compound against this herbivorous insect. However, although increases in NATA1 expression and |FRAME: CPD-13644 L-&lt;i&gt;N&lt;sup&gt;&amp;delta;&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;/i&gt;-acetylornithine| levels are triggered by the feeding of two lepidopteran herbivores, &lt;i&gt;Pieris rapae&lt;/i&gt; (white cabbage butterfly) and &lt;i&gt;Plutella xylostella&lt;/i&gt; (diamondback moth), &lt;i&gt;nata1-1&lt;/i&gt; mutants do not cause altered growth of their caterpillars |CITS: [21917546]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT2G39030-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Araport" value="AT2G39030.1" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Uniprot" value="Q9ZV05" />
